  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 223 AM EST Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Record high temperatures for the date remain on \ntrack for today; see Climate section of the forecast discussion \nfor details. Progged 925 millibar temperatures of 16 to 18 \nCelsius, near climatological maximum values, supports high \ntemperatures in the low to mid 70s with dry adiabatic lapse \nrates across our valley locations. The most unusually extreme \ntemperatures are favored for the Adirondacks and northeastern \nVermont, where the Extreme Forecast Index most recent run shows \na high confidence of near record high temperatures. This has \ntrended more extreme with time, so we have used the 90th \npercentile temperature guidance for high temperatures. The \nresulting forecast highs look consistent with recent \nverification of observed temperatures and for a scenario with \nsunshine and warm air advection from the southwest. Relative \nhumidity will remain fairly moderate based on guidance and \nobserved values upstream in the Midwest, putting a likely \nminimum in the 40% range. A few wind gusts may exceed 25 MPH \nthis morning with channeled flow in the Champlain Valley, but \nwinds aloft generally look too light to cause much concern even \nif boundary layer mixing is deeper than modeled.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A weakening cold front will put an end to our \ndry and very warm weather. Two rounds of showers appears on the \ndocket for Thursday, with the first round being relatively \ndisordered showers tied to a surge of anomalous moisture sliding\nnortheastward through our region. This precipitation looks \nstrongly forced with relatively deep moisture such that rain \nchances will be nearly certain for at least a brief period of \ntime during the daytime hours. Towards evening, while moisture \nwill be more limited, sufficiently steep lapse rates to yield a \nribbon of instability and a chance of loosely organized showers \nthat will track to the east across northern Vermont Thursday \nnight. As the front exits/washes out to our east, think flow \nwill be light enough amidst rich near surface moisture to \nsupport some patchy fog and drizzle regardless of how widespread\nthese evening showers wind up being. The lack of a wind shift \nwill keep temperatures and humidity above normal into Friday. \nHowever, conditions look mainly dry with our region sitting \nbetween an inverted trough to our east and an incoming upper \nlevel low to our west. Most likely rainfall amounts being \noffered are mainly 0.1\" to 0.33\", but with convective nature of \nsome of the rain, footprints will be quite variable.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Upper level troughing will likely settle over \neastern Canada and the Northeast as the longwave pattern is \nfavored to stall Saturday through the weekend. Model consensus \npoints to multiple rounds of rainfall associated with upper \nlevel low overhead and potential for surface low pressure to \ngyre and pivot back westward early next week while a series of \ntroughs move through the base of the longwave overhead. The \nresult will be for more shower chances Saturday onward with \ntemperatures cooling back into the 50s and upper 40s. Elevation \ndependent snowfall could occur with the cold pool expected to be\nover the region. It's feasible that there could be some lower \nelevation pellet showers should the troughs be more energetic as\nthey move through. This pattern will favor cooler overnight \nlows as well with lower elevations in the 30s and mid/upper 20s \nabove 1500-2000ft through Monday. There are some indications \nthat reinforcing cold air may occur by Tuesday possibly pushing \ntemperatures lower Tuesday night.",
